## What this run proves, and what it does not

**Proves:** the supervised loop carries a bead from kickoff to `origin/main`
without the supervisor writing any of the worker's artifacts. Capture, gate,
append, relay findings verbatim — that loop closed five times.

**Does not prove:** that the model is robust. n=1, on a bead selected for
tractability with its fix direction already named in the bead text, touching one
Go file and its test. The worker's zero BLOCK rounds are evidence about this
bead, not about the model.

## Known limitations, disclosed

- **`etude gate` alone cannot pass a gate here.** Not a defect in synthesis —
  fail-closed behaviour was correct every time — but every passing verdict on
  this run required a manual append. `etude-4ed`.
- **The bootstrap path is lossy.** Re-recorded seat entries drop `raw_output`,
  `session` and the prior round's optionals; the only `pass`-marked verify
  attempt carries no `check.0`/`check.1` seats at all, so the record certifying
  verify as passing holds zero deterministic-check evidence. Recorded on
  `etude-4ed`.
- **Two claims in the implement artifact are unverifiable** — what the plan seats
  and the verify seat said — because in-harness seat outputs are preserved
  nowhere. The supervisor's own prose is the sole record.
- **`etude gate` runs checks in the caller's cwd**, so a supervisor can certify
  the wrong worktree and see green. `etude-4qi` (P1).
- **`seats[].optional` is empty on every BOOTSTRAP attempt**, so most optionals
  exist only as prose. The precise figure is **18 of 21**: the three escalated
  `r1` attempts that `etude gate` produced natively DO record one codex optional
  each, machine-readably. An earlier draft said all 21 — true of the premise
  (bootstrap attempts carry none) but not of the run, and the distinction matters
  because it shows the native path already does the right thing.
- **Nothing detects a stage artifact edited after its gate passed.** Two of the
  five gated artifacts on `etude-3xt` drifted: `plan` was gated at `6019869…` and
  the lane's file is now `a0cc911…`; `verify` was gated at `e0f8f46…` and is now
  `d3098187…`. Both are benign in origin — the worker was folding the optionals I
  relayed — and "zero ungated stage artifacts" still holds because the ref
  preserves the reviewed bytes. But a reader opening the lane sees a document no
  seat reviewed, with no signal that it diverged. It happened twice in five, in
  the run offered as proof. Filed `etude-pq7`.
- **Two of the five gates were invoked from the SUPERVISOR's worktree**, not the
  lane: `plan.r1` and `implement.r1` record a seat-adapter path under
  `epic-9uf/`, while `verify.r1`, `docs.r1` and `review.r1` record one under
  `lane-3xt/`. Harmless here only because plan and implement carry no
  deterministic checks — which is precisely why `etude-4qi` is a live hazard
  rather than a theoretical one, and the record shows I made the mistake twice
  before recognising it.
- **The run ref does not link to the landed commit.** `etude-3xt`'s manifest has
  `refs: {}` and every stage records `git_sha 1b837a1`, the branch point. Nothing
  in the record connects the gated evidence to `752be1a`; that link exists only
  in the bead's close reason.
- **This was one lane, one worker, one bead.** Nothing here exercises two lanes
  at once, a worker that disputes a gate, or a bead that blocks repeatedly.
